# Solution

**Answer: Yes.** The infinite cyclic group $\mathbb{Z}$ has exactly one subgroup of infinite index.

## Proof

Consider $G = \mathbb{Z}$ under addition. This is an infinite group.

**Classification of subgroups.** Every subgroup of $\mathbb{Z}$ is of the form $n\mathbb{Z} = \{nk : k \in \mathbb{Z}\}$ for some integer $n \geq 0$, where $0\mathbb{Z} = \{0\}$ is the trivial subgroup. This is a standard fact: if $H \leq \mathbb{Z}$ is non-trivial, let $n = \min\{h \in H : h > 0\}$ (which exists by the well-ordering principle since $H$ contains a positive element), and one verifies $H = n\mathbb{Z}$.

**Computing indices.** For $n \geq 1$, the quotient $\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z}$ has exactly $n$ elements (the cosets $0 + n\mathbb{Z}, 1 + n\mathbb{Z}, \ldots, (n-1) + n\mathbb{Z}$), so
$$[\mathbb{Z} : n\mathbb{Z}] = n < \infty.$$
For the trivial subgroup $\{0\} = 0\mathbb{Z}$, the cosets are $\{k\}$ for each $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, so there are infinitely many cosets:
$$[\mathbb{Z} : \{0\}] = \infty.$$

**Conclusion.** The only subgroup of $\mathbb{Z}$ with infinite index is $\{0\}$. Every other subgroup $n\mathbb{Z}$ (with $n \geq 1$) has finite index $n$. Since $\mathbb{Z}$ is infinite, it is an infinite group with exactly one subgroup of infinite index. $\blacksquare$

